<Base title="Videos" description="Seminar presentations and lectures on depth psychology, addiction recovery, and the archetype of the Alcoholic by Cody Peterson.">
  <article>
    <h1>Videos</h1>
    <p>Seminars, lectures, and presentations on depth psychology, the archetype of the Alcoholic, and Jungian approaches to recovery.</p>

    <h2>Soulwork and the Twelve Steps</h2>

    <h3>Monotheism, Misogyny, and Eusebia: Recovering Reverence for the Feminine Soul in Emotional Sobriety</h3>
    <p>This talk explores how Western culture's monotheistic bias — the elevation of 'One' truth, 'One' God, 'One' ideal — has shaped our spiritual lives and our recovery programs. Drawing on Henry Corbin, James Hillman, and John Welwood, I trace how serenity became an idol that displaced the feminine image and silenced the complexity of feeling. Against this history, I turn to the Greek notion of eusebia — reverence for relationship, a posture of honoring the many daimones of the soul.</p>

    <h3>The Repression of the Feminine: Hysteria in the Big Book</h3>
    <p>In this talk, Cody continues to explore the repression of the feeling function and the restoration of feeling as a means to emotional sobriety. Here, he dives into the repression of the feminine and how hysteria has emerged in modern spirituality as an expression of Socrates's old idea of apatheia.</p>

    <h2>Dreamwork: Dreams and Symbols of Recovery</h2>
    <p>A four-part series presented for Friendly Circle Berlin, April 2025.</p>

    <h3>Dreamwork 1: Alchemical Symbolism and Modern Dreams</h3>
    <p>Cody introduces dream interpretation from the lens of Carl Jung's contributions to alchemy. Examines different ways to interpret dreams, with audience Q&amp;A.</p>

    <h3>Dreamwork 2: The Divine Feminine</h3>
    <p>Cody dives deeper into the anima, examining the Gnostic figure of Sophia, the divine feminine trapped in matter, and her relationship to the modern masculine psyche.</p>

    <h3>Dreamwork 3: Mercurius</h3>
    <p>Cody introduces the alchemical concept of Mercurius, a duplex character equated with the Trickster and the Alcoholic, and examines the relationship between Mercurius and the anima.</p>

    <h3>Dreamwork 4: The Philosopher's Stone</h3>
    <p>Cody takes us through the alchemical concept of the philosopher's stone, squaring the circle, the four functions, and mythological context for the coniunctio.</p>

    <h2>The Archetype of the Alcoholic Seminars</h2>

    <h3>Spiritus Contra Spiritum</h3>
    <p>A deep dive into the psychology behind Jung's statement Spiritus Contra Spiritum. Explores symbols associated with the coniunctio oppositorum, including the Shaman/Trickster, the archetype of the Alcoholic, and Mercurius Duplex.</p>

    <h3>The Trickster, The Shaman, and the Alcoholic</h3>
    <p>Introduction by Ian McCabe. Cody introduces his conception of the Archetype of the Alcoholic, comparing it to the ancient energy known as the Trickster/Shaman.</p>

    <h3>Myths of Expanding Consciousness: The Mythological Mycelia of the Twelve Steps</h3>
    <p>Introduction by Dr. Steven Herrmann. New historical information on Jung's trip to Taos, NM, in 1925, and how the Twelve Steps are a continuation of the concept of a personal myth.</p>

